WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.470
<v Speaker 1>Welcome to this deep dive.

2
00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:03.230
<v Speaker 1>I'm really glad you are joining us today.

3
00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:12.190
<v Speaker 1>We are going to be looking closely at Reza Shah Kazemi's spiritual ethos, specifically his essay on the mind of the commander of the faithful, peace be upon him.

4
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:15.950
<v Speaker 2>It is just a remarkable piece of work.

5
00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:19.550
<v Speaker 2>It demands a complete reorientation of how we think.

6
00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:20.350
<v Speaker 1>It really does.

7
00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:25.150
<v Speaker 1>And the deep dive will explore why the author renders the central concept of consciousness as

8
00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:30.910
<v Speaker 1>intellect, never reason, and where that single profound choice leads.

9
00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:36.750
<v Speaker 2>Because the core principle here, the absolute center of it all is this, that which has no second.

10
00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:38.830
<v Speaker 2>does not enter the category of number.

11
00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:48.030
<v Speaker 1>I think we have to establish the crisis before we can even get to that principle though, because I mean, if you look at our modern society, we have a very specific vocabulary for praising a great mind.

12
00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:48.750
<v Speaker 2>We certainly do.

13
00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:51.150
<v Speaker 2>Think about the words we instinctively reach for.

14
00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.870
<v Speaker 2>You know, we call someone highly rational.

15
00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:55.830
<v Speaker 1>Or we say they are intensely logical.

16
00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:56.590
<v Speaker 2>Precisely.

17
00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:59.150
<v Speaker 2>We compliment them for being sharp in an argument.

18
00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:03.390
<v Speaker 2>We praise the calculating mind that can weigh claims and find the most efficient outcome.

19
00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:15.470
<v Speaker 1>But using that vocabulary words like rational or logical, when trying to describe how the commander of the faithful actually perceived reality, it feels fundamentally inadequate.

20
00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:16.590
<v Speaker 2>It feels restrictive.

21
00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:16.990
<v Speaker 1>It does.

22
00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:22.590
<v Speaker 1>It feels like you are trying to force something completely boundless into this very tight modern box.

23
00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:27.390
<v Speaker 2>There is a governing analogy here that captures that failure perfectly.

24
00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:33.030
<v Speaker 2>It is like trying to gauge the depth of an ocean with a ruler meant for a desk.

25
00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:38.830
<v Speaker 1>Standard 12 inch wooden ruler and you are just standing on the shore repeatedly dipping it into the surf.

26
00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:39.550
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

27
00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.710
<v Speaker 2>And the real tragedy of that image is not just that the ruler is too short.

28
00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:47.870
<v Speaker 2>It is that reason overreaches its limits when applied to the infinite.

29
00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:50.350
<v Speaker 2>It is simply the wrong tool for the medium.

30
00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:55.310
<v Speaker 1>So if reason is that inadequate desk ruler snapping under the pressure,

31
00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.710
<v Speaker 1>What is the ocean measuring instrument?

32
00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:02.510
<v Speaker 2>Well, the author insists that we have to set aside the word reason entirely in this context.

33
00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:05.310
<v Speaker 2>We have to resurrect the classical meaning of the word intellect.

34
00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:09.630
<v Speaker 1>And we should be clear because modern English has sort of flattened those terms until they mean the same thing.

35
00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:10.670
<v Speaker 2>They really have.

36
00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:16.430
<v Speaker 2>He is not using intellect to mean cleverness or being good at standardized exams.

37
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:21.070
<v Speaker 2>He is reaching back to the Latin intellectus, which mirrors the Greek nous.

38
00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.510
<v Speaker 1>Meaning it is not just

39
00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:24.750
<v Speaker 1>a machine for processing data.

40
00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:28.750
<v Speaker 2>No, it is a direct contemplative vision of transcendent realities.

41
00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:30.430
<v Speaker 2>It is an organ of seeing.

42
00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:37.070
<v Speaker 1>Let's deconstruct that idea of seeing, actually, because the author breaks down the word understanding in a way that I found fascinating.

43
00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:41.390
<v Speaker 1>To understand a reality is to literally stand under it.

44
00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:43.310
<v Speaker 2>Is an architectural metaphor almost.

45
00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:46.470
<v Speaker 1>Right, you are not just filing trivia away in a cabinet in your head.

46
00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:51.150
<v Speaker 1>You stand under a perception and you let a shadow fall across you and govern your actions.

47
00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:52.430
<v Speaker 1>Understanding is

48
00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:54.670
<v Speaker 1>bestowed on you, not manufactured.

49
00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:56.670
<v Speaker 2>Yes, it reorganizes a life.

50
00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:02.190
<v Speaker 2>And we have to contrast that direct seeing with reason, the Latin ratio or Greek dianoia.

51
00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:04.270
<v Speaker 2>Reason is inherently indirect.

52
00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:05.230
<v Speaker 2>It is discursive.

53
00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:06.910
<v Speaker 1>Meaning it operates step by step.

54
00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:07.350
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

55
00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:08.750
<v Speaker 2>It needs to walk the entire path.

56
00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:12.510
<v Speaker 2>It relies on mathematical ratio, comparison, binary logic, you know, this, not that.

57
00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:16.270
<v Speaker 2>It arrives at a mental picture or a formula, not the reality itself.

58
00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:20.630
<v Speaker 1>I don't buy that yet isn't intellect just a fancier word for reason?

59
00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:22.550
<v Speaker 1>Aren't we just splitting hairs over

60
00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:23.710
<v Speaker 1>It is a fair.

61
00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:25.790
<v Speaker 2>Question, but no, we are not splitting hairs.

62
00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:28.030
<v Speaker 2>It is about a hierarchical relationship.

63
00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:33.310
<v Speaker 2>Reason is the instrument the intellect employs, but it is never its rival.

64
00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:35.070
<v Speaker 1>So they do different jobs.

65
00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:35.630
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

66
00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:36.910
<v Speaker 2>Think of it this way.

67
00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:39.550
<v Speaker 2>Reason brings you up to the door.

68
00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:41.630
<v Speaker 2>It defines the outward form.

69
00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:46.830
<v Speaker 2>But the intellect actually walks through the door and experiences the reality directly.

70
00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:48.590
<v Speaker 2>Reason builds the map.

71
00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:51.870
<v Speaker 2>The intellect jumps into the ocean.

72
00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:56.270
<v Speaker 1>And the modern tragedy is that we have collapsed the intellect into the calculating mind.

73
00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:59.390
<v Speaker 1>We pretend the ocean doesn't exist, only the map.

74
00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:01.070
<v Speaker 2>And that collapse fragments us.

75
00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:02.590
<v Speaker 1>I mean, look at what happens.

76
00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:09.230
<v Speaker 1>We take a person's moral bearing and their aesthetic sense of beauty and we shove them into separate rooms away from their intelligence.

77
00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:10.270
<v Speaker 2>We isolate them.

78
00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:12.750
<v Speaker 2>We treat them like they have nothing to do with one another.

79
00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:18.110
<v Speaker 1>But the author talks about the spirit of the intellect, which is this holistic perceiving rooted in the divine breath.

80
00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.390
<v Speaker 1>It totally exceeds the mere machinery of the head.

81
00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:22.110
<v Speaker 2>It does.

82
00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:24.030
<v Speaker 2>It encompasses the whole human being.

83
00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:34.990
<v Speaker 1>So having established that the intellect is this faculty of direct vision rather than a calculating machine, the question becomes, what does this look like under extreme pressure?

84
00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:36.990
<v Speaker 2>We have to look at the battlefield to see it.

85
00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:40.590
<v Speaker 2>The author pivots to the chaotic edge of the battle of the camel.

86
00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:43.870
<v Speaker 1>Paint the scene for us because the context is everything here.

87
00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:45.150
<v Speaker 2>You have armies drawn up.

88
00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:46.430
<v Speaker 2>The dust is rising.

89
00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:50.110
<v Speaker 2>The psychological pressure of imminent violence is absolute.

90
00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.270
<v Speaker 2>Survival instincts are at their peak.

91
00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.550
<v Speaker 2>And suddenly a Bedouin steps from the ranks.

92
00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:57.230
<v Speaker 1>Just a single guy stepping out.

93
00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:57.630
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

94
00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.430
<v Speaker 2>And he asks a seemingly absurd, simple question.

95
00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:02.990
<v Speaker 2>Do you say that God is one?

96
00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:04.950
<v Speaker 1>The juxtaposition is just jarring.

97
00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:09.950
<v Speaker 1>You have swords drawn and this man wants to hold a theology seminar and the ranks scold him naturally.

98
00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:13.630
<v Speaker 2>And we have to realize the ranks are operating perfectly on reason.

99
00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:16.350
<v Speaker 1>Because reason assesses the time and the place.

100
00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:16.830
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

101
00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.430
<v Speaker 2>Reason calculates utility.

102
00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:21.230
<v Speaker 2>It says we have a physical threat that theological inquiries can wait.

103
00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:22.910
<v Speaker 2>It is the completely reasonable view.

104
00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:25.310
<v Speaker 1>But the commander of the faithful stops them.

105
00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:26.830
<v Speaker 1>He defends the question.

106
00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:28.590
<v Speaker 2>He tells them to leave the man alone.

107
00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:32.590
<v Speaker 2>He says that this knowledge is exactly what he wishes for the people.

108
00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:35.710
<v Speaker 1>The order of priorities is just profound.

109
00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:42.190
<v Speaker 1>Theology, the right knowledge of ultimate reality is the fundamental intention behind any action.

110
00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:44.750
<v Speaker 2>Whether you are on a prayer rug or a battlefield.

111
00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:49.710
<v Speaker 1>There is no time where asking about the true nature of the divine is out of bounds.

112
00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:53.790
<v Speaker 2>Because the physical conflict is secondary to clarifying that oneness.

113
00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:58.910
<v Speaker 2>So right there on the battlefield, the discourse is given to the young disciple and the ranks.

114
00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:01.070
<v Speaker 2>He delivers the core principle.

115
00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:04.750
<v Speaker 2>That which has no second does not enter the category of number.

116
00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:05.870
<v Speaker 1>That sounds like wordplay.

117
00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:09.870
<v Speaker 1>How does refusing to call God one as a number change anything?

118
00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:12.030
<v Speaker 2>Well, counting is inherently fragile.

119
00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:16.110
<v Speaker 2>The moment you say something is numerically one, you imply a series.

120
00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:18.390
<v Speaker 1>You admit the possibility of 2, 3, 4.

121
00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:18.910
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

122
00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:25.550
<v Speaker 2>If God is 1 as a number, it implies a series, making the divine just one item among others in the universe.

123
00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:27.230
<v Speaker 2>A thing among things.

124
00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:29.230
<v Speaker 1>Even if it is the most powerful item on the list.

125
00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:29.550
<v Speaker 2>Right.

126
00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:31.710
<v Speaker 2>And that undoes the oneness entirely.

127
00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:34.830
<v Speaker 1>I am thinking of the early Sufi master's definition here.

128
00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:39.790
<v Speaker 1>He defined oneness as setting apart the eternal from all that was originated in time.

129
00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:40.270
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

130
00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:42.030
<v Speaker 2>They share no common measure.

131
00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:43.870
<v Speaker 2>You cannot put them on the same scale.

132
00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:46.510
<v Speaker 1>Which leads us to the dual nature of the real.

133
00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:52.350
<v Speaker 1>It is utterly transcendent, meaning it is inaccessible, it has no likeness.

134
00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:54.990
<v Speaker 2>But simultaneously it is entirely imminent.

135
00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:59.710
<v Speaker 1>To calculating reason that feels like a paradox, but to the intellect it makes sense.

136
00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:03.790
<v Speaker 1>If the real has no second, there is no outside for it to be absent from.

137
00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:06.350
<v Speaker 2>Therefore it must be manifested by all things.

138
00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:08.030
<v Speaker 2>It is impossible to lose.

139
00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:13.470
<v Speaker 2>Because it has no second, the multiplicity of the physical world does not compete with divine oneness.

140
00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:14.350
<v Speaker 2>It declares it.

141
00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:17.230
<v Speaker 1>The trees, the oceans, the dust on the battlefield.

142
00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:18.830
<v Speaker 2>They cease to be opaque facts.

143
00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:20.910
<v Speaker 2>Things become signs pointing home.

144
00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:25.390
<v Speaker 1>But if the physical world is full of signs pointing home, how do we read them?

145
00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:28.910
<v Speaker 1>If I look at a tree with just calculating reason, I just see biology.

146
00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:30.190
<v Speaker 2>We require a guide.

147
00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:33.790
<v Speaker 2>We have to journey from the outward appearance back to the first reality.

148
00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:36.190
<v Speaker 1>Which brings us to the stakes of this deep dive.

149
00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:38.430
<v Speaker 1>The concept of the inner interpretation.

150
00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:40.430
<v Speaker 2>Yes, retracing those appearances.

151
00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:43.550
<v Speaker 1>And that requires engaging with the revealed text.

152
00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:47.950
<v Speaker 1>Quran is described as a silent book inscribed between two covers.

153
00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:49.950
<v Speaker 1>It cannot speak without an interpreter.

154
00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:51.550
<v Speaker 2>And that interpreter is the intellect.

155
00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:55.070
<v Speaker 2>The revealed text requires the intellect to be spoken.

156
00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:57.630
<v Speaker 1>And this science of interpretation has a lineage.

157
00:07:57.790 --> 00:08:02.670
<v Speaker 1>The Prophet, peace, and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family taught it.

158
00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:05.710
<v Speaker 2>And figures like the prophet's cousin inherited it.

159
00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:09.710
<v Speaker 1>Showing that the inner meaning is inexhaustible once the intellect is brought to bear on it.

160
00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:11.630
<v Speaker 1>The ultimate goal being tawhid.

161
00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:16.430
<v Speaker 2>The ultimate making one of truth, leading multiplicity back to the single source.

162
00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:21.230
<v Speaker 2>But we have to steelman the strict rationalist view here.

163
00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:22.910
<v Speaker 1>We should, to be fair to their premise.

164
00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:27.310
<v Speaker 2>The rationalist looks at this and assumes intellect and revelation must exclude each other.

165
00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:29.310
<v Speaker 2>They see it as a zero-sum game.

166
00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:34.270
<v Speaker 2>You are either free and rational or bound and following revelation from the outside.

167
00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:40.430
<v Speaker 1>But if the intellect has this incredible power of direct vision, why rely on a silent text from the outside at all?

168
00:08:40.670 --> 00:08:43.870
<v Speaker 1>Doesn't submitting to revelation just shut down the intellect's freedom?

169
00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:46.190
<v Speaker 2>If you accept the rationalist dichotomy, yes.

170
00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:51.150
<v Speaker 2>But the classical Ismaili traditions reading dismantles that entirely.

171
00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:54.830
<v Speaker 2>In this traditions reading, they do not compete.

172
00:08:55.390 --> 00:08:57.390
<v Speaker 2>They exist in creative reciprocity.

173
00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:58.830
<v Speaker 1>They complete one another.

174
00:08:58.830 --> 00:08:59.630
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

175
00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:05.470
<v Speaker 2>There is an outer messenger, which is the revelation, and there is an inner messenger, which is the intellect.

176
00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:08.590
<v Speaker 2>There are two reaches of a single act of communication.

177
00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:13.550
<v Speaker 1>The outer messenger speaks and the inner messenger recognizes the voice.

178
00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:15.790
<v Speaker 2>Because they issue from the same source.

179
00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:18.590
<v Speaker 1>So it is recognition, not blind obedience.

180
00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:22.910
<v Speaker 1>But why does the inner intellect perfectly recognize the outer revelation?

181
00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:24.190
<v Speaker 1>Where did they meet before?

182
00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:26.030
<v Speaker 2>Well, they met before this physical world.

183
00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:28.110
<v Speaker 2>Revelation acts as a reminder.

184
00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:30.190
<v Speaker 2>You cannot be reminded of something you never knew.

185
00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:33.990
<v Speaker 1>That makes me think of the primordial testimony from the chapter of The Heights.

186
00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:35.070
<v Speaker 2>Am I not your Lord?

187
00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:35.630
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

188
00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:41.070
<v Speaker 1>The human spirit witnessed the truth before this world, but we are born into a state of spiritual amnesia.

189
00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:42.910
<v Speaker 2>We are distracted by multiplicity.

190
00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:46.990
<v Speaker 2>To understand this, imagine a physician surrounding a man who has lost his memory.

191
00:09:46.990 --> 00:09:48.510
<v Speaker 1>Like a traumatic brain injury.

192
00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:49.310
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

193
00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:51.390
<v Speaker 2>The man doesn't know who he is.

194
00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:54.110
<v Speaker 2>The physician doesn't force him to memorize a new biography.

195
00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:57.710
<v Speaker 2>He tells the family to bring in familiar tokens from his past.

196
00:09:57.950 --> 00:09:59.390
<v Speaker 2>A watch, a photograph.

197
00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:00.750
<v Speaker 1>Items he used to know.

198
00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:03.630
<v Speaker 2>The tokens don't forcefully insert new memories.

199
00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:05.630
<v Speaker 2>They wake up the dormant mind.

200
00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:09.070
<v Speaker 2>They resonate with what is already sleeping beneath the trauma.

201
00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:11.710
<v Speaker 2>Revelation is that set of tokens.

202
00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:12.990
<v Speaker 1>Okay, I see it now.

203
00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:14.230
<v Speaker 1>I concede that point.

204
00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:18.670
<v Speaker 1>It's not about an external authority forcing a new rule on a blank slate.

205
00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:23.070
<v Speaker 1>It's an alarm clock waking up a truth we already possess but forgot.

206
00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:23.950
<v Speaker 2>It is an awakening.

207
00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:25.630
<v Speaker 2>The truth was already yours.

208
00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:28.990
<v Speaker 1>Which brings us to what feels like the climax of this teaching.

209
00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:32.510
<v Speaker 1>There is a phrase from the collection of Ali's sermons that perfectly anchors this.

210
00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:37.510
<v Speaker 1>He says, the prophets were sent to unearth the buried treasures of the intellect.

211
00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:38.910
<v Speaker 2>To unearth the buried treasures.

212
00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:39.390
<v Speaker 2>The wealth

213
00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:40.270
<v Speaker 2>is already present.

214
00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:43.670
<v Speaker 2>The author elaborates on this with three powerful images.

215
00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:44.510
<v Speaker 1>Let's walk through those.

216
00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:45.550
<v Speaker 1>The first one is the farmer.

217
00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:47.550
<v Speaker 2>The farmer waters a hidden seed.

218
00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:49.870
<v Speaker 2>He doesn't manufacture the life of the plant.

219
00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:55.390
<v Speaker 2>He just provides the water, the revelation, to coax out the life waiting in the dark earth.

220
00:10:55.550 --> 00:10:57.230
<v Speaker 1>And the second is the miner.

221
00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:58.990
<v Speaker 2>Digging for locked gold.

222
00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:01.630
<v Speaker 2>The miner doesn't invent the gold.

223
00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:04.910
<v Speaker 2>He endures the hard labor to bring it up into the light.

224
00:11:05.150 --> 00:11:06.510
<v Speaker 2>The gold was always gold.

225
00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:07.950
<v Speaker 1>And the third is the diver.

226
00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:10.190
<v Speaker 2>Descending to the seabed for pearls.

227
00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:15.550
<v Speaker 2>The pearls are useless in the crushing depth until someone makes the terrifying descent to retrieve them.

228
00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:20.430
<v Speaker 1>In all three, the farmer, the miner, the diver, the work is retrieval.

229
00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:21.870
<v Speaker 1>It is not manufacture.

230
00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:25.630
<v Speaker 2>And that brings us back to the absolute limit of the calculating mind.

231
00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:28.670
<v Speaker 2>Reason is honored for bringing us to the edge.

232
00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:30.190
<v Speaker 2>It gathers the facts.

233
00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:31.470
<v Speaker 2>It brings us to the door.

234
00:11:31.550 --> 00:11:34.390
<v Speaker 1>But it falters before the reality that has no second.

235
00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:37.150
<v Speaker 2>Because reason works by relating one thing to another.

236
00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:39.470
<v Speaker 2>Rumi warned us about this limit.

237
00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:44.670
<v Speaker 2>He said, the leg of the reasoners is a wooden leg, and a wooden leg is exceedingly infirm.

238
00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:47.870
<v Speaker 1>The final step requires the awakened spirit of the intellect.

239
00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:50.110
<v Speaker 1>It requires us to plunge into the ocean.

240
00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:59.070
<v Speaker 2>We have seen how reason fails, how the intellect sees directly, how oneness is beyond number on the battlefield, and how revelation unearths our buried treasures.

241
00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:02.510
<v Speaker 1>That which has no second does not enter the category of number.

242
00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:04.070
<v Speaker 1>So as we wrap up,

243
00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:06.350
<v Speaker 1>I want you to look at your own life.

244
00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:18.990
<v Speaker 1>Which certainties are you currently trying to argue or calculate from the outside using the exhausting mechanics of reason when they might instead be directly seen and remembered from within using the quiet vision of your intellect?

245
00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:23.150
<v Speaker 1>What is 1 habit of attention you can adopt this week that begins to change that approach?

246
00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:24.750
<v Speaker 1>We will leave that hanging in the air for you.
